  "live_records": {
    "q-94649": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94649",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Let $N$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=0.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The constant function $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in R$ belongs to $N.$\n\nII. For any $f(t),g(t)\\in N,$ we have $f(t)-g(t)=g(t)-f(t).$\n\nIII. The addition of functions in $N$ is commutative.",
      "worked_solution": "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $N$ contains only functions that evaluate to zero at zero. However, the function defined by $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in R$ satisfies $f(0)=1\\ne 0.$ So, $f\\notin N.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider functions $f(t)=2t$ and $g(t)=t$ in $N.$ Then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)-g(t) & =2t-t \\\\  & =t \\\\ g(t)-f(t) & =t-2t \\\\  & =-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(t)-g(t)\\ne g(t)-f(t).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(t),g(t)\\in N,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)=g(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nsince the addition of functions is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
