    "q-106807": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106807",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Let $P$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ such that ${a}_{22}=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. For any $A,B\\in P$ and any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (A+B)=\\alpha A+\\alpha B.$\n\nII. The scalar multiplication of matrices in $P$ is associative.\n\nIII. For any $A\\in P,$ we have $1\\cdot A={I}_{2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ and $B=({b}_{ij})$ from $P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha ({a}_{ij}+{b}_{ij})=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\alpha {b}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha (A+B)=\\alpha A+\\alpha B.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matric $A=({a}_{ij})$ from $P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta {a}_{ij})=(\\alpha \\beta ){a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta A)=(\\alpha \\beta )A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ from $P.$ Then,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot 0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\ne {I}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
